  "Question": "A square and a circle have the same area. What is the ratio of the side length of the square to the radius of the circle?",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\( \\ \\dfrac{\\sqrt{\\pi}}{2} \\)",
    "B": "\\( \\ \\sqrt{\\pi} \\)",
    "C": "\\( \\ \\pi \\)",
    "D": "\\( \\ 2\\pi \\)",
    "E": "\\( \\ \\pi^{2} \\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Solution": "The equal areas let us connect the two lengths using their area formulas. Let \\(s>0\\) be the square's side length and \\(r>0\\) the circle's radius, measured in the same units. Then\n\\[\ns^2=\\pi r^2.\n\\]\nBecause \\(r>0\\), we can divide by \\(r^2\\) to obtain\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{s}{r}\\right)^2=\\pi.\n\\]\nThe ratio \\(s/r\\) is positive, so it is the positive square root of \\(\\pi\\). Thus the requested ratio is \\(\\boxed{\\sqrt{\\pi}}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{B}}\\).",
